/usr/bin/include from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for include, so this is the disassembly, and include.c is
that disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f include.ctl include.dist

include.dist is the binary off inode 25 of disks/dist/1013-8_dist_3.IMD - the
third distribution disk, not the second where most of the text tools are -
7788 bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It is a Whitesmith a.out: 99 94,
text at 0100 for 18dd bytes and data at 2000, in the older runtime that login
and td have, c.ent at 1982 and c.ent2 at 198d.  The program is the one of that
name in Kernighan and Plauger's Software Tools.

Its own code is five functions: main at 0111, include at 012f, getword at
0225, the error at 02b4 and a one-line fputs at 02dd.  The strings are
"include" at 0122, "read" at 012a and ": can't read" at 02a6.

main calls include with a null name, which is the standard input, and exits.
include opens its file - "read", through fopen at 02f4 - or takes the
standard input, and goes round a loop: read a line with fgets (0712, handed
512), look at the end of file, and take the first word of the line with
getword.  If the word is "include" the next word is a name and include is
called with it, recursively; otherwise the line is written as it is.  A name
that is empty is not opened and not an error.  At the end of the file it is
closed, unless it is the standard input.

getword is not print's.  It looks alike - blanks and controls, then a run of
printable characters - and it skips the first and does not copy them, so the
word comes back without them and "   include   file" is an include.

THINGS THAT ARE NOT WHAT THE MANUAL SAYS

The end of the file is tested after the line is read, and fgets sets it when
it meets the end at all, so the last line of a file that has no newline on it
has set the flag and is not looked at: it is not written, and if it is an
include it is not done.  include.c keeps this, in getl.

Lines are fgets's: 511 characters at the most.  A line of 600 is two, and a
second piece that begins "include" is an include.

The buffers for the line and the word are single and shared by every level of
an include.  Nothing is the worse for it, because a level reads its next line
over the last.

The streams are the library's sixteen, three of them the standard three, so
that a fourteenth file open at once is not to be had, and a file that includes
itself writes thirteen levels of itself and then "name: can't read".
This tree's stdio has twelve streams and would stop at ten.  include.c does
not use stdio for the files: it reads with open and read, a buffer to a level,
and counts, so that it is thirteen here as well.

The exit status is 1 for a good run and 0 for a file that would not open: the
inverted pair, as newuser's and td's.  include.c exits 0 and 1.

Checked in usersim against the original, byte for byte, standard error and all:
a file that includes a file that includes a file, with indented includes and
names followed by comments; "included", "INCLUDE" and an include with no name,
which are not; tabs between the word and the name; lines of 300, 500 and 600
characters with an include in the middle of one; a file with no newline at the
end of it, at the top and in an include; carriage returns; bytes over 0177;
a file that is missing, and one that includes itself.
